On 15/08/10 07:04, Ronald Humperdinck wrote:
> Will there be a new official version of NetHack?  Or are Un and Spork the 
> real future for the game? 
> 
> http://www.spod-central.org/~psmith/nh/anhftime.html shows that the longest 
> wait between releases prior to 2003 was a couple years. Does the bug list 
> being updated from time to time by some apathetic souls really still give 
> anyone hope that a real release will ever occur? It's nearly 2011.

Don't expect anything here. No one but the Devteam knows, and their
information policy is to not announce any release dates or plans.
Occasionally they drop some hint; e.g. about the actual engagement,
but what I've heard 16 months ago doesn't fill me with hope; the
most promising part of the message was: "We haven't given up.", and,
frankly, that is not much.

If you're interested in a (more?) stable version to play with, the
NAO server seems to me to be very reliable (and the service is very
good - thanks Pasi, Drew, et al.!); I think they've applied patches
to fix some bugs.

Otherwise, if you want something new, I'd go for the variants that
you mentioned. They have some very nice features and a couple tweaks.
Assuming there's a new Vanilla version available at some time, it will
certainly have to compete and catch up with those variants.

Whether those variants are "the future", I don't know; software that
heavily depends on just one person (development but mainly design) is
not the most reliable basis for a long lasting development path that
deserves the prediction "it's the future". (I seem to recall that e.g.
the level compiler enhancements have been implemented by Pasi(?) and
probably others contributed, too. Derek and Patric, resp., may answer
it more accurately who's involved in which tasks of the development
and design process.) Folks get involved in their Real World tasks and
we will have to expect a similar decline as in Nethack, probably in a
even shorter time span in case of a smaller development/design team.

